  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on comparing the minimum probabilities of the lotteries and prefers the one with the lower minimum probability. In this case, Lottery B has a minimum probability of 20% (for the 0 Euro outcome), while Lottery A has a minimum probability of 100% (for its only outcome). The reason would thus prefer Lottery B. The individual also shows a preference for Lottery B, motivated by the desire to retain a chance of no loss — that is, focusing on the 20% probability of avoiding loss. This aligns with attention to the lowest probability outcome (0 loss with 20%), which is exactly the information used by the decision reason. Although the individual does not explicitly state they are comparing minimum probabilities across lotteries, their focus on the existence of a small chance to avoid loss mirrors the core idea of the reason. The justification is consistent with being influenced by the presence of a low-probability favorable outcome, which is structurally aligned with the decision rule. However, the individual does not indicate a general rule based on minimizing the minimum probability; instead, the emphasis is on the psychological value of avoiding sure loss. Therefore, while the information considered overlaps significantly (the 20% chance of zero loss), the justification is more outcome-focused (avoiding sure loss) than rule-based (comparing minimum probabilities). This suggests partial alignment but not full adherence to the formal decision reason.",
